<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Hris.Modules.Workflow
    Phase 3 (Workforce Management), Sprint 2, per IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md
    ("Both attendance correction and leave application require approval routing.
    Building either before workflow means hardcoding approvals and revisiting them
    later.").

    Source specification: docs/04-modules/workflow/.

    This module is NOT the Workflow Engine. Hris.Foundation.WorkflowEngine (Phase 1,
    Sprint 5) executes running instances: task creation, assignment, completion,
    escalation timers firing, the SLA clock, retry, and dead-lettering. This module
    owns the business semantics those mechanics operate on: which processes require
    approval, who approves, in what order, what the escalation policy is, and how
    long an SLA allows. IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md's own Phase 3 Epic table flags the
    confusion explicitly ("do not confuse with Phase 1's Workflow Engine"), and
    workflow/README.md's own boundary table is the authority on the split. The
    parallel it draws: administration records who holds what authority, the
    Authorization Framework evaluates decisions from it.

    Three Aggregate Roots, exactly as domain/aggregates.md states with no scoping
    ambiguity: WorkflowDefinition (a tenant-authored process, its ordered steps as
    child entities, and its publication lifecycle), ApprovalPolicy (tenant-level
    approval governance, one per tenant), and ApprovalDelegation (a time-bounded
    transfer of approval authority between two users). WorkflowStep is a child
    entity of WorkflowDefinition, never its own root, because publication validates
    the step graph as a whole; a separately persistable step would let a valid
    published definition be edited into an invalid one with nothing checking.

    Workflow instances and approval tasks are deliberately absent, per that same
    document: they change on the engine's schedule rather than the tenant's, and a
    copy of them here would be a lagging second record of state the engine already
    owns authoritatively.

    This module's own root namespace segment is "Workflow", and none of its three
    Aggregate Roots is named Workflow, so the CS0118 name-resolution collision
    Organization, Position, Employment, and Employee each hit does not recur here,
    the same as Administration before it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Hris.Modules.Workflow</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" Version="12.4.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" Version="11.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions" Version="11.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="9.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="9.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BuildingBlocks\Hris.SharedKernel\Hris.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BuildingBlocks\Hris.Application\Hris.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BuildingBlocks\Hris.Infrastructure\Hris.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
